5. COLLISION
------------------------------------------------------------

A collision happens when different keys produce the same index.

Example:

    12 % 10 = 2
    22 % 10 = 2
    32 % 10 = 2

All three values want index 2.

This is called a collision.



6. COLLISION HANDLING
------------------------------------------------------------

Two common techniques are:

    1. Chaining
    2. Open Addressing


For beginners, understand chaining first.


Example:

    index 2 -> 12 -> 22 -> 32


A simple representation:

    vector<vector<int>> table(10);


Example code:

#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {

    int SIZE = 10;

    vector<vector<int>> table(SIZE);

    int x = 27;

    int index = x % SIZE;

    table[index].push_back(x);

    return 0;
}



7. C++ STL HASH TABLES
------------------------------------------------------------

C++ already provides hash table data structures.

The two most important ones are:

    unordered_map
    unordered_set



8. UNORDERED_MAP
------------------------------------------------------------

unordered_map stores:

    KEY -> VALUE

Syntax:

    unordered_map<key_type, value_type> mp;


Example:

    unordered_map<int, int> mp;


Here:

    key   = integer
    value = integer


Example:

    mp[10] = 100;
    mp[20] = 200;

So:

    10 -> 100
    20 -> 200



9. FREQUENCY USING UNORDERED_MAP
------------------------------------------------------------

This is one of the most important hashing patterns.

Example:

    10 20 10 30 20 10

We want:

    10 -> 3
    20 -> 2
    30 -> 1


Code:

#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {

    vector<int> a = {10, 20, 10, 30, 20, 10};

    unordered_map<int, int> freq;

    for (int x : a) {
        freq[x]++;
    }

    for (auto p : freq) {
        cout << p.first << " -> " << p.second << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}


IMPORTANT PATTERN:

    unordered_map<int, int> freq;

    for (int x : a) {
        freq[x]++;
    }


Remember this pattern.



10. UNORDERED_MAP OPERATIONS
------------------------------------------------------------

Create:

    unordered_map<int, int> mp;


Insert:

    mp[10] = 100;


Access:

    cout << mp[10];


Update:

    mp[10]++;

    mp[10] += 5;


Delete:

    mp.erase(10);


Size:

    cout << mp.size();


Clear:

    mp.clear();



11. CHECK WHETHER A KEY EXISTS
------------------------------------------------------------

Method 1: find()

    if (mp.find(x) != mp.end()) {
        cout << "Found";
    }


If key exists:

    mp.find(x) != mp.end()


If key doesn't exist:

    mp.find(x) == mp.end()



12. COUNT()
------------------------------------------------------------

We can also use:

    mp.count(x)


For unordered_map:

    0 -> key does not exist
    1 -> key exists


Example:

    if (mp.count(20)) {
        cout << "Exists";
    }


This is very useful in competitive programming.



13. ITERATING OVER UNORDERED_MAP
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    unordered_map<int, int> mp;

    mp[10] = 2;
    mp[20] = 5;
    mp[30] = 1;

    for (auto  p : mp) {
        cout << p.first << " " << p.second << '\n';
    }


Here:

    p.first  -> key
    p.second -> value


IMPORTANT:

unordered_map does NOT maintain sorted order.



14. UNORDERED_SET
------------------------------------------------------------

Sometimes we only care whether a value exists.

We don't need a key-value pair.

Use:

    unordered_set


Example:

    unordered_set<int> st;


Insert:

    st.insert(10);
    st.insert(20);
    st.insert(30);


Check:

    if (st.count(20)) {
        cout << "Exists";
    }


Delete:

    st.erase(20);



15. SET VS UNORDERED_SET
------------------------------------------------------------

set:

    set<int> st;

Properties:

    - Sorted
    - O(log N)


unordered_set:

    unordered_set<int> st;

Properties:

    - Not sorted
    - O(1) average



16. MAP VS UNORDERED_MAP
------------------------------------------------------------

map:

    map<int, int> mp;

Properties:

    - Keys are sorted
    - O(log N)


unordered_map:

    unordered_map<int, int> mp;

Properties:

    - Keys are not sorted
    - O(1) average


Remember:

    map           -> sorted
    unordered_map -> hashing
    set           -> sorted unique values
    unordered_set -> hash-based unique values



17. WHEN TO USE UNORDERED_SET?
------------------------------------------------------------

Use unordered_set when you only need:

    "Have I seen this value?"

Examples:

    - Check duplicates
    - Check existence
    - Find distinct elements
    - Track visited values



18. WHEN TO USE UNORDERED_MAP?
------------------------------------------------------------

Use unordered_map when you need:

    - Frequency
    - Position
    - Mapping
    - Previously seen information


Common patterns:

    Frequency:

        unordered_map<int, int> freq;

        freq[x]++;


    Position:

        unordered_map<int, int> pos;

        pos[x] = i;



19. EXAMPLE: CHECK DUPLICATES
------------------------------------------------------------

Problem:

Given an array, check whether any value appears more than once.

Example:

    1 2 3 4 2

Answer:

    YES


Solution:

#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {

    vector<int> a = {1, 2, 3, 4, 2};

    unordered_set<int> st;

    for (int x : a) {

        if (st.count(x)) {
            cout << "YES\n";
            return 0;
        }

        st.insert(x);
    }

    cout << "NO\n";

    return 0;
}


Complexity:

    Time  -> O(N * N) average -> jhut bol rha h
    Space -> O(N)



